## The gap

Revision 1 said the host opens `git-reply` `O_RDWR` and writes
`{ id, status, stdout, stderr }\n` per request. It never said *how*. The cell
is untrusted and may never drain that pipe. Once it fills, a synchronous host
write stops the runner's event loop, so its deadlines and teardown can never
fire; an asynchronous stream write queues replies in host memory without
bound. A cell that spams small requests, each earning an error frame, reaches
one or the other. Revision 1's hazard list named the symmetric read hazard —
a blocking `openSync(fifo, "r")` — and missed the write. That is a spec
defect at a trust boundary, and it is mine.

## The measurement

Run 2026-10-03 in this session's sandbox, retained in
`~/.bisellium-evidence/W-132-spec/`:

```
m11-reply-writes.mjs     FIFO capacity                                65536 bytes
                         writeSync on O_RDWR|O_NONBLOCK when full     throws EAGAIN
                         writeSync on blocking O_RDWR, 1 MiB, full    never returns (killed at 4s)
m11b-shortwrite.mjs      256 KiB non-blocking write, 8192 free        returns 8192
                         256 KiB non-blocking write, 16384 free       returns 16384
m11d-held-remainder.mjs  1 MiB frame, reader drains                   delivered intact, 58 retries
                         1 MiB frame, never drained                   65536 written, one frame held
                         close() after either                         exit {code: 0, signal: null}
```

Two of these decide the rule. The refusal is `EAGAIN`, not a partial-success
illusion, so a non-blocking fd gives the host a clean "later". And a short
write is real — 256 KiB into 16384 free bytes returns exactly 16384 — so a
reply cannot be dropped on a short write without tearing the stream, and a
legitimate reply can be far larger than the pipe, since the broker already
caps a `git` stdout at 4 MiB (`run-builder-host.mjs:298`). Dropping would
have been the smaller rule and would have broken honest large replies.

## The rule

`git-reply` opens `O_RDWR | O_NONBLOCK`; `git-request` is unchanged. A reply
is written with `writeSync` from an offset while the call makes progress. On
`EAGAIN` or a zero write, the remainder is kept and the host **pauses the
request stream** until it is delivered, retrying from an `unref`ed
`setTimeout` — never a blocking write, and never an asynchronous `fs.write`,
whose pending threadpool request is itself a live handle that would keep the
runner from exiting.

The bound is **one reply frame**, at most the existing 4 MiB stdout cap:
because no request is read while a remainder is pending, a second can never
exist. A flooding cell fills `git-request`, a kernel buffer, and is blocked or
refused there — the correct backpressure, and its own problem. Nothing is
dropped, no run is ended, no new deadline is added. The stall reaches the cell
as its own client deadline, exactly as a crashed client already does.
`close()` discards the remainder, clears the timer and closes both handles;
measured on both the drained and the never-drained path, the host exits 0.

## The row

Behaviour 4 gains sub-row 4a, not a fifth behaviour: the red for behaviour 4
is the absent `scripts/git-broker.mjs`, and that is 4a's red too, so
`studio/ci/reds/W-132/04.log` stands as recorded. 4a is prescribed against the
harness that already exists in `packages/commands/src/run.test.ts` — the host
in a child process under `--expose-gc`, `host.command("sample")` for retained
memory, `host.served()`, and the `close()` exit assertion. The new cell
scenario is `deaf`: it holds `git-reply` open and never reads it, writes
well-formed frames to a non-blocking `git-request` until `EAGAIN` or a 5000
cap, and exits. Non-blocking is what makes the scenario terminate, and the
refusal is itself the backpressure the rule claims.

Five assertions follow, in order: the host still answers `sample`, which is
the one that fails if a reply write blocks; `host.served()` is below the
frames admitted and unchanged across two samples, which separates "stopped
reading" from "queued"; retained growth stays under the 8 MiB bound the row
already uses, so no new bound is introduced; after the test drains
`git-reply`, a fresh sentinel is answered and `served` grows again, proving a
pause and not a death; and a second `deaf` run leaves a remainder pending so
the row's existing `close()` assertion runs under the hostile condition.
Fixed messages only, and no cell output in any message — the replay filter
rules of revision 1 are unchanged.

